necolor\":\"white\",\"ticks\":\"\"},\"caxis\":{\"gridcolor\":\"white\",\"linecolor\":\"white\",\"ticks\":\"\"}},\"coloraxis\":{\"colorbar\":{\"outlinewidth\":0,\"ticks\":\"\"}},\"colorscale\":{\"sequential\":[[0.0,\"#0d0887\"],[0.1111111111111111,\"#46039f\"],[0.2222222222222222,\"#7201a8\"],[0.3333333333333333,\"#9c179e\"],[0.4444444444444444,\"#bd3786\"],[0.5555555555555556,\"#d8576b\"],[0.6666666666666666,\"#ed7953\"],[0.7777777777777778,\"#fb9f3a\"],[0.8888888888888888,\"#fdca26\"],[1.0,\"#f0f921\"]],\"sequentialminus\":[[0.0,\"#0d0887\"],[0.1111111111111111,\"#46039f\"],[0.2222222222222222,\"#7201a8\"],[0.3333333333333333,\"#9c179e\"],[0.4444444444444444,\"#bd3786\"],[0.5555555555555556,\"#d8576b\"],[0.6666666666666666,\"#ed7953\"],[0.7777777777777778,\"#fb9f3a\"],[0.8888888888888888,\"#fdca26\"],[1.0,\"#f0f921\"]],\"diverging\":[[0,\"#8e0152\"],[0.1,\"#c51b7d\"],[0.2,\"#de77ae\"],[0.3,\"#f1b6da\"],[0.4,\"#fde0ef\"],[0.5,\"#f7f7f7\"],[0.6,\"#e6f5d0\"],[0.7,\"#b8e186\"],[0.8,\"#7fbc41\"],[0.9,\"#4d9221\"],[1,\"#276419\"]]},\"xaxis\":{\"gridcolor\":\"white\",\"linecolor\":\"white\",\"ticks\":\"\",\"title\":{\"standoff\":15},\"zerolinecolor\":\"white\",\"automargin\":true,\"zerolinewidth\":2},\"yaxis\":{\"gridcolor\":\"white\",\"linecolor\":\"white\",\"ticks\":\"\",\"title\":{\"standoff\":15},\"zerolinecolor\":\"white\",\"automargin\":true,\"zerolinewidth\":2},\"scene\":{\"xaxis\":{\"backgroundcolor\":\"#E5ECF6\",\"gridcolor\":\"white\",\"linecolor\":\"white\",\"showbackground\":true,\"ticks\":\"\",\"zerolinecolor\":\"white\",\"gridwidth\":2},\"yaxis\":{\"backgroundcolor\":\"#E5ECF6\",\"gridcolor\":\"white\",\"linecolor\":\"white\",\"showbackground\":true,\"ticks\":\"\",\"zerolinecolor\":\"white\",\"gridwidth\":2},\"zaxis\":{\"backgroundcolor\":\"#E5ECF6\",\"gridcolor\":\"white\",\"linecolor\":\"white\",\"showbackground\":true,\"ticks\":\"\",\"zerolinecolor\":\"white\",\"gridwidth\":2}},\"shapedefaults\":{\"line\":{\"color\":\"#2a3f5f\"}},\"annotationdefaults\":{\"arrowcolor\":\"#2a3f5f\",\"arrowhead\":0,\"arrowwidth\":1},\"geo\":{\"bgcolor\":\"white\",\"landcolor\":\"#E5ECF6\",\"subunitcolor\":\"white\",\"showland\":true,\"showlakes\":true,\"lakecolor\":\"white\"},\"title\":{\"x\":0.05},\"margin\":{\"b\":0,\"l\":0,\"r\":0,\"t\":30}}},\"xaxis\":{\"anchor\":\"y\",\"domain\":[0.0,1.0],\"title\":{\"text\":\"grid points n²\"},\"type\":\"log\"},\"yaxis\":{\"anchor\":\"x\",\"domain\":[0.0,1.0],\"title\":{\"text\":\"forward time [s]\"},\"type\":\"log\"},\"legend\":{\"title\":{\"text\":\"implementation\"},\"tracegroupgap\":0},\"title\":{\"text\":\"Forward time vs grid points (K = 100)\"}},                        {\"responsive\": true}                    )                };            </script>        </div>\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n## What this shows\n\n**The sequential sweep on the CPU.** At $n = 512$ the lexicographic gradient takes\n325\\.7 ms with Enzyme and\n19\\.7 s with JAX, although their forward\npasses are close (177\\.6 ms and\n517\\.8 ms). Enzyme's gradient costs\n1\\.8×\nits forward pass; JAX's costs\n38×.\nJAX expresses the sweep as nested `lax.scan`s, and the transposed scan of a scan does\nmuch more work per step than the scalar loop it came from.\n\n**The sequential sweep on the GPU.** At $n = 64$ the lexicographic gradient takes\n7\\.3 s with JAX and\n8\\.3 s with Reactant, against\n4\\.1 ms for Enzyme on one CPU core. Every\nscalar update becomes a step of an XLA loop, which on the GPU means a device round trip\nper grid point. Reactant traces the same Julia triple loop as Enzyme, so the language is\nnot the issue: the loop is slow once it goes through XLA.\n\n**Memory does not grow with K, for either tool.** We expected reverse mode through\n$K$ in-place sweeps to store $K$ copies of $u$. It doesn't, because each update is\n*linear*: its derivative is the constant $\\tfrac14$, so the backward pass needs no\nstored values. Both Enzyme's activity analysis and JAX's linearisation see this.\n\n::: {#8242efe7 .cell execution_count=5}\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=5}\n```{=html}\n<style type=\"text/css\">\n</style>\n<table id=\"T_9a8f5\">\n  <caption>Gradient memory [MiB], lexicographic, n = 256 (Enzyme: bytes allocated by the call; JAX: XLA temp buffers)</caption>\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th class=\"index_name level0\" >impl</th>\n      <th id=\"T_9a8f5_level0_col0\" class=\"col_heading level0 col0\" >enzyme</th>\n      <th id=\"T_9a8f5_level0_col1\" class=\"col_heading level0 col1\" >jax</th>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th class=\"index_name level0\" >K</th>\n      <th class=\"blank col0\" >&nbsp;</th>\n      <th class=\"blank col1\" >&nbsp;</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <th id=\"T_9a8f5_level0_row0\" class=\"row_heading level0 row0\" >10</th>\n      <td id=\"T_9a8f5_row0_col0\" class=\"data row0 col0\" >1.52</td>\n      <td id=\"T_9a8f5_row0_col1\" class=\"data row0 col1\" >2.04</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th id=\"T_9a8f5_level0_row1\" class=\"row_heading level0 row1\" >100</th>\n      <td id=\"T_9a8f5_row1_col0\" class=\"data row1 col0\" >1.52</td>\n      <td id=\"T_9a8f5_row1_col1\" class=\"data row1 col1\" >2.04</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th id=\"T_9a8f5_level0_row2\" class=\"row_heading level0 row2\" >1000</th>\n      <td id=\"T_9a8f5_row2_col0\" class=\"data row2 col0\" >1.52</td>\n      <td id=\"T_9a8f5_row2_col1\" class=\"data row2 col1\" >2.04</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nA nonlinear sweep, such as nonlinear Gauss–Seidel with a local Newton step, would need\nstored values in both tools and make memory a real issue. That is not tested here.\n\n## Where JAX wins / what we did not do\n\n::: {.callout-note}\n**Red-black on the GPU is where the XLA tools shine.** At $n = 1024$ the red-black\ngradient takes 16\\.8 ms with Reactant,\n20\\.0 ms with JAX and\n21\\.9 ms with Enzyme through\nKernelAbstractions; on small grids Enzyme is several times slower. Enzyme launches two\nkernels per sweep, forward and reverse, while XLA compiles the whole loop into one\nprogram. That launch overhead dominates on small\ngrids.\n:::\n\n- A JAX expert can rewrite the lexicographic sweep without a scalar loop. Along each\n  column the update is a first-order linear recurrence, which\n  `jax.lax.associative_scan` evaluates in parallel. That is a different algorithm, with\n  different rounding and $O(\\log n)$ depth, and you have to know the trick.\n- No Enzyme multithreading and no fused multi-sweep GPU kernel.\n\n",
